{"cuisine/floribbean-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/floribbean-cuisine","n":"Floribbean cuisine","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Southern American cuisine","cuisine/cuisine-of-the-southern-united-states"],["Fusion cuisine","cuisine/fusion-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in United States of America","intro":[["Floribbean cuisine refers to a fusion cuisine found in Florida with an emphasis on fresh regional ingredients and complex medleys of spices, especially strong flavors offset by milder ones. Floribbean-style cooking incorporates an exotic spice pantry: red curry, lemongrass, ginger, and scallions are as commonly used today in Floribbean cookery as grits and cobbler are in other parts of Florida. Foundationally, its bedrock is Conch, Black, Spanish and Cuban regional cooking, with heavy Asian influences."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"floribbean-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Floribbean_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Southern American cuisine","cuisine/cuisine-of-the-southern-united-states"],["Fusion cuisine","cuisine/fusion-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Norman Van Aken's Florida Kitchen","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Norman Van Aken","note":"Florida cooking from the Key West chef closely tied to the Floribbean style.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9780813054506","suggested":true,"isbn":"9780813054506"}]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Southern American cuisine","cuisine/cuisine-of-the-southern-united-states"],["Fusion cuisine","cuisine/fusion-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas","img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/f/fa/Sweet_Potato_Crusted_Salmon_on_Salad_with_Ranch_Style_Dressing.jpg/500px-Sweet_Potato_Crusted_Salmon_on_Salad_with_Ranch_Style_Dressing.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Mangocity","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sweet_Potato_Crusted_Salmon_on_Salad_with_Ranch_Style_Dressing.jpg"}},"cuisine/north-korean-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/north-korean-cuisine","n":"North Korean cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Korean cuisine","cuisine/korean-cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of North Korea","intro":[["North Korean cuisine is the traditional culinary practices and dishes of North Korea. Its foundations are laid by the agricultural and nomadic traditions in southern Manchuria and the Korean Peninsula. Some dishes are shared with South Korea; however, availability and quality of Northern cuisine is much more significantly affected by sociopolitical class divides."],["Historically, Korean cuisine has evolved through centuries of social and political change. Originating in ancient agricultural and nomadic traditions in southern Manchuria and the Korean Peninsula, it has gone through a complex interaction of the natural environment and different cultural trends. Rice dishes and kimchi are staple Korean foods. In a traditional meal, they accompany both side dishes (panch'an) and main courses like chuk (porridge), pulgogi (grilled meat) or myŏn (noodles). Soju liquor is the best-known traditional Korean spirit."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"north-korean-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_Korean_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Dishes","count":5,"links":[["Injo-gogi-bap","dish/injo-gogi-bap"],["Paensae","dish/paensae"],["Pyeonsu","dish/pyeonsu"],["Pyongyang Raengmyon","dish/pyongyang-raengmyon"],["Sungeo-guk","dish/sungeo-guk"]]},{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Korean cuisine","cuisine/korean-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Bap","ingredient/bap"],["Buckwheat","ingredient/buckwheat"],["Noodle","ingredient/noodle"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Sweet potato","ingredient/sweet-potato"],["Flathead grey mullet","ingredient/flathead-grey-mullet"],["Trout","ingredient/trout"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Korean cuisine","cuisine/korean-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/6/63/Cuisine_of_North_Korea_01.jpg/500px-Cuisine_of_North_Korea_01.jpg","w":500,"h":333,"by":"Suez (sophia)","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cuisine_of_North_Korea_01.jpg"}},"cuisine/awadhi-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/awadhi-cuisine","n":"Awadhi cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Uttar Pradesh cuisine","cuisine/cuisine-of-uttar-pradesh"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in India","intro":[["Awadhi cuisine is a cuisine native to the Awadh region in northern India and southern Nepal.  Awadhi cuisine can be divided into traditional Awadhi cuisines and Nawabi cuisines. The cooking styles of Lucknow are similar to those of Central Asia, the Middle East, and northern India and Western India with the cuisine comprising both vegetarian and non-vegetarian dishes. The Awadh region has been influenced by Mughal cooking techniques and the cuisine of Lucknow, which bears similarity to those of Central Asia, Kashmir, Punjab and Hyderabad. The city is also known for its Nawabi foods."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"awadhi-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Awadhi_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Dishes","count":1,"links":[["Sheermal","dish/sheermal"]]},{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Archana's Kitchen (Oriya)","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"archanaskitchen.com","note":"Lucknow and Awadhi recipes on Archana Doshi's Indian recipe site.","url":"https://archanaskitchen.com/tag/lucknow-awadhi-cuisine-recipes","suggested":true,"kind":"site"}]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Cardamom","ingredient/cardamom"],["Ghee","ingredient/ghee"],["Maida (flour)","ingredient/maida-flour"],["Maida flour","ingredient/maida-flour"],["Milk","ingredient/milk"],["Saffron","ingredient/saffron"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Uttar Pradesh cuisine","cuisine/cuisine-of-uttar-pradesh"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/d/db/Sheermal_Lucknow.JPG/500px-Sheermal_Lucknow.JPG","w":500,"h":225,"by":"Pranaykuma12","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sheermal_Lucknow.JPG","of":"Sheermal"}},"dish/arem-arem":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/arem-arem","n":"Arem-arem","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Indonesian cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Arem-arem is an Indonesian-Javanese compressed rice cake in the form of a cylinder wrapped inside a banana leaf, filled with diced vegetables, tempeh, or oncom, and eaten as a snack. It is sometimes also filled with minced meat or abon (beef floss). Arem-arem is often described as a smaller size lontong with fillings, so it is sometimes also called lontong isi (lit. 'filled lontong')."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-arem-arem"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arem-arem"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":6,"links":[["Banana leaf","ingredient/banana-leaf"],["Carrot","ingredient/carrot"],["Ground meat","ingredient/ground-meat"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Ragout","ingredient/ragout"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Daily Cooking Quest","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"dailycookingquest.com","note":"Indonesian home cook Anita's blog with a large archive of Indonesian recipes.","url":"https://dailycookingquest.com/cuisines/indonesian.html","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Indonesian cuisine","links":[["Arsik","dish/arsik"],["Asam pedas","dish/asam-pedas"],["Ayam goreng","dish/ayam-goreng"],["Ayam goreng kalasan","dish/ayam-goreng-kalasan"],["Ayam panggang","dish/ayam-panggang"],["Ayam Taliwang","dish/ayam-taliwang"],["Bajigur","dish/bajigur"],["Bakakak hayam","dish/bakakak-hayam"],["Bakpia","dish/bakpia"],["Bakwan","dish/bakwan"],["Bekasang","dish/bekasang"],["Betutu","dish/betutu"],["Bika ambon","dish/bika-ambon"],["Binte biluhuta","dish/binte-biluhuta"],["Bir pletok","dish/bir-pletok"],["Bolu kukus","dish/bolu-kukus"],["Botok","dish/botok"],["Brem","dish/brem"],["Bubur kacang hijau","dish/bubur-kacang-hijau"],["Bubur ketan hitam","dish/bubur-ketan-hitam"],["Bubur pedas","dish/bubur-pedas"],["Buntil","dish/buntil"],["Burgo (food)","dish/burgo-food"],["Cendol","dish/cendol"],["Cenil","dish/cenil"],["Cilok","dish/cilok"],["Cimpa","dish/cimpa"],["Corn fritter","dish/corn-fritter"],["Coto makassar","dish/coto-makassar"],["Dadar gulung","dish/dadar-gulung"],["Dadiah","dish/dadiah"],["Dengke mas naniura","dish/dengke-mas-naniura"],["Empal gentong","dish/empal-gentong"],["Emping","dish/emping"],["Es buah","dish/es-buah"],["Es doger","dish/es-doger"],["Es goyobod","dish/es-goyobod"],["Gado-gado","dish/gado-gado"],["Geplak","dish/geplak"],["Gepuk","dish/gepuk"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/4/48/Arem-arem.JPG/500px-Arem-arem.JPG","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Midori","lic":"CC BY 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arem-arem.JPG"}},"ingredient/maize-meal":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/maize-meal","n":"Maize meal","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 2 cuisines","intro":[["Mielie meal, also known as mealie meal or maize meal, is a relatively coarse flour or cornmeal (much coarser than cornflour or cornstarch) made from maize (also called mealies) in Southern Africa. It was originally brought to Africa from the Americas by the Portuguese. Its etymology is uncertain, and may be from the Portuguese milho, or the Afrikaans mielie, from obsolete Dutch milie (\"millet, maize\"). It is also known by various other indigenous language names depending on the locality or country."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-maize-meal"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maize_meal"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["Colombian cuisine","cuisine/colombian-cuisine",[["Arepa","dish/arepa"]]],["Venezuelan cuisine","cuisine/venezuelan-cuisine",[["Arepa","dish/arepa"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Cornmeal","ingredient/cornmeal"],["Maize flour","ingredient/maize-flour"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/5/51/Meilie_Meal.jpg/500px-Meilie_Meal.jpg","w":500,"h":301,"by":"Aleph500Adam","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Meilie_Meal.jpg"}},"ingredient/game-meat":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/game-meat","n":"Game meat","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Game or quarry are wild animals which are hunted for animal products (primarily meat), for recreation (\"sporting\"), or for trophies. The species of animals hunted as game varies in different parts of the world and by different local jurisdictions, though most are terrestrial mammals and birds. Fish caught non-commercially (recreational fishing) are also referred to as game fish."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-game-meat"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Game_meat"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine",[["Brunswick stew","dish/brunswick-stew"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Bean","ingredient/bean"],["Maize","ingredient/maize"],["Tomato","ingredient/tomato"]]}]},"dish/cheesesteak":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/cheesesteak","n":"Cheesesteak","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from American cuisine","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["A cheesesteak is a sandwich made from thinly sliced pieces of beefsteak (often rib eye) and melted cheese in a long hoagie roll. A popular regional fast food, it has its roots in the American city of Philadelphia, Pennsylvania."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-cheesesteak"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cheesesteak"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":3,"links":[["Beefsteak","ingredient/beefsteak"],["Hoagie roll","ingredient/hoagie-roll"],["Processed cheese","ingredient/processed-cheese"]]},{"h":"More from American cuisine","links":[["Angel food cake","dish/angel-food-cake"],["Banana bread","dish/banana-bread"],["Banana pudding","dish/banana-pudding"],["Barbecue sauce","dish/barbecue-sauce"],["Beer cake","dish/beer-cake"],["Better than sex cake","dish/better-than-sex-cake"],["Blini","dish/blini"],["Blooming onion","dish/blooming-onion"],["Brown Betty (dessert)","dish/brown-betty-dessert"],["Brunswick stew","dish/brunswick-stew"],["Buffalo wing","dish/buffalo-wing"],["Butter cake","dish/butter-cake"],["Caesar salad","dish/caesar-salad"],["Caramel apple","dish/caramel-apple"],["Carbonara","dish/carbonara"],["Chicago-style hot dog","dish/chicago-style-hot-dog"],["Chicken nugget","dish/chicken-nugget"],["Chiffon cake","dish/chiffon-cake"],["Chili con carne","dish/chili-con-carne"],["Chimichanga","dish/chimichanga"],["Chow-chow (food)","dish/chow-chow-food"],["Chowder","dish/chowder"],["Cioppino","dish/cioppino"],["Clam chowder","dish/clam-chowder"],["Clam dip","dish/clam-dip"],["Club sandwich","dish/club-sandwich"],["Cobb salad","dish/cobb-salad"],["Cobbler (food)","dish/cobbler-food"],["Cook's Corner","dish/cook-s-corner"],["Crab cake","dish/crab-cake"],["Croffle","dish/croffle"],["Da