:"Recipe section of the nonprofit My Jewish Learning, covering Ashkenazi, Sephardi and Mizrahi home cooking and holiday food.","url":"https://www.myjewishlearning.com/recipes/","suggested":true,"kind":"site"}]},{"h":"More from Jewish cuisine","links":[["Appetizing store","dish/appetizing-store"],["Bagel","dish/bagel"],["Brisket (Jewish dish)","dish/brisket-jewish-dish"],["Cebularz","dish/cebularz"],["Challah","dish/challah"],["Forshmak","dish/forshmak"],["Gefilte fish","dish/gefilte-fish"],["Gondi (food)","dish/gondi-food"],["Hamantash","dish/hamantash"],["Holishkes","dish/holishkes"],["Jewish deli","dish/jewish-deli"],["Lekach","dish/lekach"],["Mafrum","dish/mafrum"],["Matzah brei","dish/matzah-brei"],["Miodownik","dish/miodownik"],["Pizza dolce di Beridde","dish/pizza-dolce-di-beridde"],["Reuben sandwich","dish/reuben-sandwich"],["Shirin polo","dish/shirin-polo"],["Sorrel soup","dish/sorrel-soup"],["Teiglach","dish/teiglach"],["Tsvikli","dish/tsvikli"],["Tzimmes","dish/tzimmes"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/8/8f/Shkvarki.jpg/500px-Shkvarki.jpg","w":500,"h":471,"by":"User:Zserghei","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shkvarki.jpg"}},"dish/joulutorttu":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/joulutorttu","n":"Joulutorttu","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Finnish cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["A joulutorttu is a Finnish Christmas pastry. It is traditionally made from puff pastry in the shape of a star or pinwheel and filled with prune jam and often dusted with icing sugar. The pastries can be made into other shapes and other jams can be used in place of the prune jam."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-joulutorttu"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joulutorttu"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Finnish cuisine","cuisine/finnish-cuisine"]]},{"h":"More from Finnish cuisine","links":[["Blodpalt","dish/blodpalt"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Egg butter","dish/egg-butter"],["Glögg","dish/glogg"],["Kalakukko","dish/kalakukko"],["Karelian hot pot","dish/karelian-hot-pot"],["Kesäkeitto","dish/kesakeitto"],["Kringle","dish/kringle"],["Kuivaliha","dish/kuivaliha"],["Lanttulaatikko","dish/lanttulaatikko"],["Lohikeitto","dish/lohikeitto"],["Lörtsy","dish/lortsy"],["Semla","dish/semla"],["Smörgåstårta","dish/smorgastarta"],["Strong beer","dish/strong-beer"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/f/f1/Joulutorttuja.jpg/500px-Joulutorttuja.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Mysid","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joulutorttuja.jpg"}},"ingredient/lokshen":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/lokshen","n":"Lokshen","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Lokshen, also known as Itriyot (Hebrew: איטריות), locshen, lockshen, or Jewish egg noodles, is the common name of a range of Ashkenazi Jewish egg noodles that are commonly used in a variety of Jewish dishes including chicken soup, kugel, kasha varnishkes, lokshen mit kaese, and as a side dish to Jewish brisket, sweet and sour meat balls, apricot chicken, and many other dishes. They may also be served with melted butter or a simple tomato sauce. In the United States, lokshen are also used as the basis for various casseroles and baked dishes including tuna noodle casserole, and both sweet and savory lokshen kugels."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-lokshen"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lokshen"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Ashkenazi Jewish cuisine","cuisine/ashkenazi-jewish-cuisine",[["Kugel","dish/kugel"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Apple","ingredient/apple"],["Challah","ingredient/challah"],["Cornmeal","ingredient/cornmeal"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Matzah","ingredient/matzah"],["Noodles","ingredient/noodles"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"]]}]},"ingredient/habanero":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/habanero","n":"Habanero","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["The habanero is a pungent cultivar of Capsicum chinense chili pepper. Unripe habaneros are green, and they color as they mature. The most common color variants are orange and red, but the fruit may also be white, brown, yellow, green, or purple. Typically, a ripe habanero is 2–6 centimetres (3⁄4–2+1⁄4 inches) long. Habanero chilis are very hot, rated 100,000–350,000 on the Scoville scale. The habanero's heat, flavor, and floral aroma make it a common ingredient in hot sauces and other spicy foods."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-habanero"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Habanero"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["British cuisine","cuisine/british-cuisine",[["Phall","dish/phall"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Carolina Reaper","ingredient/carolina-reaper"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Ginger","ingredient/ginger"],["Scotch bonnet","ingredient/scotch-bonnet"],["Tomato","ingredient/tomato"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/8/86/Habanero_closeup_edit2.jpg/500px-Habanero_closeup_edit2.jpg","w":500,"h":841,"by":"The original uploader was Fir0002 at English Wikipedia.","lic":"CC BY 2.5","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.5","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Habanero_closeup_edit2.jpg"}},"ingredient/oecophylla-smaragdina":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/oecophylla-smaragdina","n":"Oecophylla smaragdina","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 3 cuisines","intro":[["Oecophylla smaragdina (common names include Asian weaver ant, weaver ant, green ant, green tree ant, and orange gaster) is a species of arboreal ant found in tropical Asia and Australia. These ants form colonies with multiple nests in trees, each nest being made of leaves stitched together using the silk produced by the ant larvae, hence the specific name oecophylla (Greek for \"leaf-house\")."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-oecophylla-smaragdina"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oecophylla_smaragdina"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":3,"groups":[["Jharkhandi cuisine","cuisine/jharkhandi-cuisine",[["Red ant chutney","dish/red-ant-chutney"]]],["Odia cuisine","cuisine/odia-cuisine",[["Red ant chutney","dish/red-ant-chutney"]]],["Oriya cuisine","cuisine/oriya-cuisine",[["Red ant chutney","dish/red-ant-chutney"]]]]}]},"dish/rigojancsi":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/rigojancsi","n":"Rigójancsi","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Hungarian cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Rigójancsi is a traditional Hungarian cube-shaped chocolate sponge cake and chocolate cream pastry. It gained popularity in the former Austria-Hungary and is named after Rigó Jancsi (1858–1927), a famous Hungarian Gypsy (Romani people) violinist."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-rigojancsi"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rig%C3%B3jancsi"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Hungarian cuisine","cuisine/hungarian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":11,"links":[["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Chocolate","ingredient/chocolate"],["Dark rum","ingredient/dark-rum"],["Egg white","ingredient/egg-white"],["Egg yolk","ingredient/egg-yolk"],["Icing","ingredient/icing"],["Jam","ingredient/jam"],["Sponge cake","ingredient/sponge-cake"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Vanilla","ingredient/vanilla"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Zsuzsa Is in the Kitchen","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"zsuzsaisinthekitchen.blogspot.com","note":"Hungarian-born home cook's blog of traditional Hungarian recipes.","url"